<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Games! – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Games!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher.
reading_2: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids.
phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize basic familiar words related to personal preferences (e.g., 'math' or 'like') when supported with illustrations or visual aids (e.g., recognizing the word 'math' while looking at a picture of numbers).
Can participate in choral reading by recognizing and producing the beginning sounds of familiar words related to personal preferences, such as 'science,' 'music,' or 'art' (e.g., identifying and producing the /s/ sound for the letter 'S' in 'science' during group reading).
Can recognize familiar letters in words related to personal preferences, such as 'math' and 'like' (e.g., recognizing the letter 'M' in 'math' and the letter 'L' in 'like' when supported with pictures).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>
                This stage activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's theme using engaging, multi-sensory activities for Kinder students.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing large flashcards of school subjects and activities previously introduced (e.g., 'math', 'music', 'art', 'playground', 'science'). As each flashcard is shown, the teacher says the word clearly, and students chorally repeat. The teacher then asks, "Do you like math?" and encourages students to respond with "Yes, I like math!" or "No, I don't like math!" using thumbs up/down gestures. This re-engages students with familiar vocabulary and the concept of preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the reading action by showing a flashcard with a picture (e.g., a child counting) and the written word "math" below it. The teacher points to the word "math" while saying it, then points to the letter 'M' in 'math' and says its sound, /m/. The teacher repeats this with "games", pointing to the word and then the letter 'G' and its sound /g/. The teacher demonstrates linking the visual word, its sound, and the initial letter sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'math' flashcard) "Is this 'music' or 'math'?" (Expected: 'math')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to letter 'M' in 'math') "What letter is this?" (Expected: 'M')</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                Students are exposed to new language and content in an authentic context, fostering global comprehension. The focus is on reading a text with familiar vocabulary and supporting visuals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated big book or projected story titled "My Favorite School Day" or "What I Like at School!" The story features short sentences, large print, and clear pictures related to school subjects and activities like 'math', 'music', 'art', 'science', 'playground', and 'games'. Example sentences: "I like school! I like to count in math. I like to sing in music. I like to draw in art. I like to play games in the playground."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognize familiar words and letters in a simple, illustrated story related to school preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the entire story aloud, pointing to each word as they read, emphasizing key vocabulary. After reading, the teacher asks, "What is the story about? School or animals?" (Expected: 'School'). "Does the boy/girl like school?" (Expected: 'Yes').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word-Picture Recognition:</b> The teacher re-reads each page slowly. For each key word (e.g., 'math'), the teacher pauses, points to the written word and its corresponding picture, and asks, "What word is this?" or "What do you see?" Students respond chorally or individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter Spotting:</b> On pages featuring 'math' and 'games', the teacher asks students to "Find the letter 'M' in 'math'!" or "Find the letter 'G' in 'games'!" encouraging them to point to the letters in the big book.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled practice of the target language, emphasizing accuracy in reading comprehension and recognition of words and letters.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading familiar words (math, music, art, games, school) and recognizing specific letters (M, L, G, S). Grammar: Subject + verb ('I like...').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-Picture Matching Worksheet:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple pictures of school activities (e.g., a child singing for 'music', a child drawing for 'art', a child playing for 'games') on one side and the corresponding words written clearly on the other side. Students draw a line from each picture to its matching word. The teacher circulates, providing support and naming the pictures and words aloud. (Focus on 'music', 'art', 'science', 'math', 'games').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Letter Spotting and Circling:</b> Using the same worksheet or a new one, the teacher instructs students to look at the words. The teacher calls out a letter (e.g., "Circle the letter 'M' in 'math'!") and students identify and circle it. Repeat with other letters in target vocabulary, such as 'L' in 'like' and 'G' in 'games'. The teacher guides them by pointing to the letter on a large display if needed. This reinforces phonemic awareness by focusing on specific letter recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Choral Word Reading & Teacher Check:</b> The teacher displays flashcards with only the words (e.g., 'math', 'music', 'games'). Students chorally read each word. The teacher then points to a word and asks an individual student or small group to identify it. For example, "What does this say?" (pointing to 'games'). The teacher provides immediate feedback and affirmation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                Students use the target language in a communicative task within a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and completing the task. This stage involves active reading engagement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Choral reading of a "I Like School!" chant with corresponding picture prompts and word cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher prepares large sentence strips or a poster with a simple, repetitive chant about school activities, each line accompanied by a relevant picture and the corresponding word highlighted. The chant incorporates target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Chant:</b>
                <br>Teacher: "I like school, yes I do!" (Points to picture of school, then 'school' word)
                <br>Students: (Chorally read and point) "I like school, yes I do!"
                <br>Teacher: "I like math, one, two, three!" (Points to picture of counting, then 'math' word)
                <br>Students: (Chorally read and point) "I like math, one, two, three!"
                <br>Teacher: "I like music, sing with me!" (Points to picture of singing, then 'music' word)
                <br>Students: (Chorally read and point) "I like music, sing with me!"
                <br>Teacher: "I like games, happy me!" (Points to picture of playing, then 'games' word)
                <br>Students: (Chorally read and point) "I like games, happy me!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher leads the choral reading, ensuring students point to the words as they read, connecting the written word to the spoken word and the visual. The teacher encourages clear pronunciation of /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games' by modeling and having students repeat. This activity directly addresses "Participate in choral reading sessions" and "Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids," and "Recognize familiar letters" by actively tracing the words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                This stage evaluates learning through formative assessment and encourages student self-reflection, ensuring learning outcomes are met.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the choral reading and a simple worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three boxes. Each box contains a picture of a school activity (e.g., 'math', 'music', 'games') and two word options below it (e.g., under the 'math' picture: 'math' / 'art'). Students circle the word that matches the picture. Additionally, one box asks students to circle a specific letter (e.g., "Circle the letter 'L' in 'like'").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes at least 2 out of 3 target words by circling the correct word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly circles the specified letter.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively in choral reading (observed by the teacher).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Thumbs up/down) "Can I find the word 'math'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Thumbs up/down) "Can I find the letter 'M'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>
                This final stage allows both students and the teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I read today?" (e.g., 'math', 'games', 'music')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which letter did I find in a word?" (e.g., 'M', 'L', 'G')</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The visual support and repetition were very effective for word recognition. Students engaged well with pointing to words during choral reading. Some students still need more practice with specific letter sound association for /s/ in 'science'. I need to provide more individual support for letter recognition. The "I Like School!" chant worked well to reinforce 'I like...' grammar. For the next lesson (Speaking), I will focus on encouraging students to orally express their preferences about school subjects and games using simple sentences, building on the recognized words from today's reading session.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>